WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.269 --> 00:00:03.690
Speaker 1: Let's get back to the phones and
speak to a few more people, you can join the

00:00:03.690 --> 00:00:09.369
discord through which we take phone calls
at David Pakman dot com slash discord.

00:00:09.369 --> 00:00:10.760
It's a great system.

00:00:10.760 --> 00:00:12.650
It really, really is.

00:00:12.650 --> 00:00:17.750
Why don't we go next to Dean from the state
of Ohio, Dean?

00:00:17.750 --> 00:00:19.960
You were on the air.

00:00:19.960 --> 00:00:21.690
Hello.

00:00:21.690 --> 00:00:23.660
You hear me?

00:00:23.660 --> 00:00:25.170
Yes, I can.

00:00:25.170 --> 00:00:26.170
Speaker 5: Oh, my first time caller.

00:00:26.170 --> 00:00:27.170
Thank you.

00:00:27.170 --> 00:00:28.170
Glad to talk to you, David.

00:00:28.170 --> 00:00:32.740
Pleasure and I'm a current college student
and they put a basically a Campsie class,

00:00:32.740 --> 00:00:33.740
a comparative politics.

00:00:33.740 --> 00:00:40.040
And I was curious, in your opinion on parliamentary
democracies, because right now in the US,

00:00:40.040 --> 00:00:45.040
we have kind of a very strong gridlock in
our Senate system.

00:00:45.040 --> 00:00:49.450
And I'm not obviously, I prefer presidential
systems, but you know, if we had a parliamentary

00:00:49.450 --> 00:00:53.720
democracy where we could, I guess, effectively
get more things done, do you think the U.S.

00:00:53.720 --> 00:00:55.720
would be a better or worse country for it?

00:00:55.720 --> 00:00:59.690
It's kind of a broad question, but my
Speaker 1: general belief on this and I've

00:00:59.690 --> 00:01:04.430
talked about it a little bit before, is that
I think that a parliamentary system would

00:01:04.430 --> 00:01:12.310
bring actually many good things to the United
States where you would have, you know, a system

00:01:12.310 --> 00:01:18.499
where you have the leader of the party with
the greatest representation in the Legislature

00:01:18.499 --> 00:01:19.499
becoming.

00:01:19.499 --> 00:01:23.619
Typically, it would be like the prime minister
or the chancellor rather than a directly elected

00:01:23.619 --> 00:01:26.009
president.

00:01:26.009 --> 00:01:31.560
I generally think it would be an improvement
for the United States, although I have had

00:01:31.560 --> 00:01:39.590
some concerns that there is a lot of really
crazy extremists here and that really horrible

00:01:39.590 --> 00:01:44.770
parties which have no official representation
right now could end up having some official

00:01:44.770 --> 00:01:46.119
representation in that.

00:01:46.119 --> 00:01:48.419
There's certainly a danger to that.

00:01:48.419 --> 00:01:52.359
But, you know, it's a very hypothetical.

00:01:52.359 --> 00:01:56.259
It's merely an academic question because the
US is not going to change to a parliamentary

00:01:56.259 --> 00:01:57.259
system anytime soon.

00:01:57.259 --> 00:02:01.889
I'm not like Trump or I go the U.S. will never
at no point soon, right?

00:02:01.889 --> 00:02:02.889
At no point soon.

00:02:02.889 --> 00:02:07.429
And so I think that we have to try to improve
things in other in other ways.

00:02:07.429 --> 00:02:13.520
Of course, yeah, but no, I like parliamentary
systems as a general principle.

00:02:13.520 --> 00:02:18.340
Speaker 5: And I guess as a slight addendum
to that question.

00:02:18.340 --> 00:02:23.050
We have a mix in the US of a single member
districts that we elected to represent people,

00:02:23.050 --> 00:02:24.770
and then we have a proportional system.

00:02:24.770 --> 00:02:25.770
Yes.

00:02:25.770 --> 00:02:26.770
Do you think we would be better off?

00:02:26.770 --> 00:02:28.100
We went to 100 percent proportional.

00:02:28.100 --> 00:02:33.260
I know there's pros and cons of both, but
it just seems like we have a Senate that disproportionately

00:02:33.260 --> 00:02:37.350
represents Republicans, even though they have
a less percentage overall in the United States.

00:02:37.350 --> 00:02:38.350
Yeah.

00:02:38.350 --> 00:02:41.450
Speaker 1: Look, the Senate is fundamentally
anti-democratic.

00:02:41.450 --> 00:02:42.880
I've talked about this before.

00:02:42.880 --> 00:02:44.070
It's not representative.

00:02:44.070 --> 00:02:49.880
You have something like the senators representing.

00:02:49.880 --> 00:02:51.730
I'm trying to get the number right.

00:02:51.730 --> 00:02:54.830
It's like 34 percent of the population.

00:02:54.830 --> 00:03:00.450
Senators representing 34 percent of the population
control half of the votes, which is fundamentally

00:03:00.450 --> 00:03:02.090
unrepresentative and undemocratic.

00:03:02.090 --> 00:03:07.040
Again, it's one of these things where there
is no way to deny that that's the case, and

00:03:07.040 --> 00:03:09.420
it's just not going to change any time soon.

00:03:09.420 --> 00:03:13.730
And so I wonder about the.

00:03:13.730 --> 00:03:17.890
The prudence of focusing on it, I want to
make sure people understand this.

00:03:17.890 --> 00:03:23.280
The Senate is fundamentally not Democratic
and not representative, but OK, so then what

00:03:23.280 --> 00:03:26.960
do we do with that and do we just focus on
that and ignore everything else that we could

00:03:26.960 --> 00:03:27.960
be working on?

00:03:27.960 --> 00:03:29.380
That doesn't seem prudent, either.

00:03:29.380 --> 00:03:35.150
Speaker 5: Oh, but of course, David, my apologies,
I will try to come if I call again next time

00:03:35.150 --> 00:03:37.600
to get a more pragmatic question.

00:03:37.600 --> 00:03:38.600
I was more of an actor.

00:03:38.600 --> 00:03:40.480
Speaker 1: No, no, I'm not criticizing that.

00:03:40.480 --> 00:03:45.870
I'm only making it like, in other words, I'm
saying you are 100 percent spot on about these

00:03:45.870 --> 00:03:49.410
issues and I agree with you and I talk about
them as well.

00:03:49.410 --> 00:03:54.290
And I wonder, what is the balance of like
activism capital that should be spent on one

00:03:54.290 --> 00:03:55.530
thing versus the other?

00:03:55.530 --> 00:03:59.510
When these things seem very ingrained now,
the counterpoint would be David, if you don't

00:03:59.510 --> 00:04:02.360
try to activate around these things, they're
going to remain the way they are.

00:04:02.360 --> 00:04:03.460
And that's a good point, too.

00:04:03.460 --> 00:04:09.819
You know, so I don't know where our pragmatic
versus idealist balance should be.

00:04:09.819 --> 00:04:10.819
Is a much David.

00:04:10.819 --> 00:04:11.819
My pleasure.

00:04:11.819 --> 00:04:13.240
All right.

00:04:13.240 --> 00:04:15.090
Extraordinary phone call there.

00:04:15.090 --> 00:04:16.410
Love it.

00:04:16.410 --> 00:04:25.330
Let's go next to Michael from Berlin, New
Jersey.

00:04:25.330 --> 00:04:26.990
Michael, your next.

00:04:26.990 --> 00:04:29.039
Speaker 3: Hi, how are you doing?

00:04:29.039 --> 00:04:32.650
Speaker 1: I'm doing all right, you sound
like you're talking through a voice modulator.

00:04:32.650 --> 00:04:37.850
Speaker 3: I know I had say,
Speaker 1: OK, what's going on?

00:04:37.850 --> 00:04:46.340
Speaker 3: I just wanted to, I guess, explain
the experience at the polls yesterday, I know

00:04:46.340 --> 00:04:53.919
I worked as a poll worker, OK in New Jersey,
and I just wanted to explain my experience

00:04:53.919 --> 00:04:56.240
that it was actually a great experience.

00:04:56.240 --> 00:05:00.249
I felt like I was really doing a Democratic
thing.

00:05:00.249 --> 00:05:01.249
OK.

00:05:01.249 --> 00:05:05.479
Unfortunately, it didn't work out the way
I wanted it.

00:05:05.479 --> 00:05:10.680
I wanted it to, but but it's OK because I
felt like I actually did my duty.

00:05:10.680 --> 00:05:12.889
So were the man actually some curious?

00:05:12.889 --> 00:05:18.150
Speaker 1: Were there poll workers trying
to election-year in some way during during

00:05:18.150 --> 00:05:19.150
their work?

00:05:19.150 --> 00:05:21.830
Speaker 3: Yeah, we did actually have the
problem.

00:05:21.830 --> 00:05:26.540
They were within the one 100 foot zone and
people were complaining outside.

00:05:26.540 --> 00:05:30.942
Speaker 1: But those were poll workers or
these were just people, people trying to campaign

00:05:30.942 --> 00:05:31.942
